<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Four Communes</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1946">1946</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Four Communes of Senegal</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Four Communes were the colonial towns of Saint-Louis, Gorée, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rufisque">Rufisque</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dakar-senegal">Dakar</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a>, whose inhabitants, the originaires, enjoyed French civil and political rights, unlike the subjects of the rest of French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>. Given municipal status between 1872 and 1887, they elected a deputy to the French parliament, from 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blaise-diagne">Blaise Diagne</a>, whose laws confirmed their citizenship. Their special status ended when citizenship was extended across the empire in 1946.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A small minority, the originaires of the <b>Four Communes</b> of Saint-Louis, Gorée, Rufisque and Dakar, held French citizenship, confirmed by laws of 1915 and 1916 sponsored by Blaise Diagne, and elected a deputy to Paris.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-west-africa">French West Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Four Communes</b> of Saint-Louis, Goree, Rufisque and Dakar gave their inhabitants French citizenship, and in 1914 they elected Blaise Diagne, the first black African in the French parliament.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-senegal">French Senegal</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1872 it became one of the <b>Four Communes of Senegal</b>, whose inhabitants gained French citizenship rights.</blockquote><a href="/state/goree">Gorée</a></li></ul></section>
